1
00:00:02,461 --> 00:00:03,675
written on lead among

2
00:00:03,715 --> 00:00:05,710
spells and human remains.

3
00:00:06,014 --> 00:00:08,377
The historian Tacitus says Germanicus

4
00:00:08,417 --> 00:00:10,644
was still alive when they were discovered.

5
00:00:11,041 --> 00:00:13,637
Germanicus was the emperor's adopted son,

6
00:00:13,677 --> 00:00:16,507
sent east with authority over Rome's governors.

7
00:00:16,649 --> 00:00:19,140
But Piso, the governor of Syria,

8
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:20,884
overturned his orders.

9
00:00:21,188 --> 00:00:23,017
When Germanicus fell ill,

10
00:00:23,057 --> 00:00:25,377
he believed Piso had poisoned him.

11
00:00:25,588 --> 00:00:27,746
The hidden lead sheets were curses,

12
00:00:27,829 --> 00:00:28,753
meant to deliver him

13
00:00:28,793 --> 00:00:30,335
to the gods below.

14
00:00:30,639 --> 00:00:31,949
Before he died,

15
00:00:32,032 --> 00:00:33,652
Germanicus begged his friends

16
00:00:33,692 --> 00:00:35,733
to bring Piso to justice.

17
00:00:36,037 --> 00:00:37,123
So in Rome,

18
00:00:37,163 --> 00:00:37,994
Piso was put

19
00:00:38,034 --> 00:00:39,898
on trial before the Senate,

20
00:00:39,938 --> 00:00:42,386
accused of poisoning Germanicus.

21
00:00:42,690 --> 00:00:44,043
Before the verdict,

22
00:00:44,083 --> 00:00:45,892
Piso was found dead,

23
00:00:45,975 --> 00:00:47,610
a sword beside him.

24
00:00:47,914 --> 00:00:50,149
The Senate condemned him anyway,

25
00:00:50,189 --> 00:00:52,030
and ordered its decree displayed

26
00:00:52,070 --> 00:00:54,170
in bronze across the empire.

27
00:00:54,473 --> 00:00:57,595
Copies of that decree survive in Spain.

28
00:00:57,805 --> 00:01:00,273
They condemn Piso for corrupting soldiers

29
00:01:00,313 --> 00:01:02,482
and trying to start a civil war.

30
00:01:02,995 --> 00:01:04,345
But not for murder.
